-- Mass gap: the Lean library of the paper (research/paper/v3); the map from paper statements to declarations is research/FORMALIZATION.md
import MassGap.WilsonHypercubic
import MassGap.WilsonBridge  -- The join: the entropy read applied to the constructed SU(N) Wilson correlation, not an opaque one
import MassGap.LatticeGauge    -- residual A / step A1: a genuine finite lattice gauge measure over a compact group; the OS invariances DERIVED from Haar-invariance ∘ plaquette-permutation (Symmetry.expect_invariant : ⟨O∘reindex⟩=⟨O⟩), not rfl; foundation-only
import MassGap.CompactGauge    -- step A2a: compact Hausdorff Borel group ⇒ canonical probability Haar (probHaar); the A1 measure DERIVED (not assumed); expect_invariant_haar, foundation-only
import MassGap.SUN             -- step A2b: the compact topological-group + Borel instances for SU(N)=specialUnitaryGroup (Fin n) ℂ (Nonempty, IsTopologicalGroup, CompactSpace via closed∩Tychonoff, BorelSpace); su_expect_invariant lands the derived invariance on the real SU(N) Haar measure; foundation-only
import MassGap.WilsonAction    -- dynamical face (brick 1): the genuine Wilson action density φ_W(g)=1−(1/N)Re tr g on SU(N); wilsonDensity_one (zero at identity) + wilsonDensity_conj (gauge-invariance seed, from trace cyclicity); foundation-only
import MassGap.WilsonLattice   -- dynamical face (brick 2): real plaquette holonomy = ordered loop product (boundary word of (link,orientation)); wilsonSystem + wilsonSymmetry (compat = List.map/prod reindexing, the "lattice rotation permutes plaquettes" content) + wilson_expect_invariant (A1 payoff on a real action); foundation-only
import MassGap.WilsonReal      -- dynamical face (brick 2b): a concrete SU(2) two-plaquette Wilson system (genuine 4-link loops, real wilsonDensity action) with a ℤ/2 plaquette-swap symmetry (hbd2 by decide); sysReal_invariant = Euclidean/permutation invariance of the real Wilson correlation, derived; foundation-only
import MassGap.ReflectionPositivity  -- the mechanism of reflection positivity: a positive-half function paired with its own reflection integrates to a square (proved); the cited half is writing the Wilson weight in paired form
import MassGap.Reflect  -- the reflection of the hypercubic lattice, which the tree had no way to write down: WilsonHypercubic supplies only the axis permutations, and reflection positivity is a statement about a map the Symmetry structure cannot express. Two things force that. A tau-link spans [x_tau, x_tau+1], so its mirror is based at c-1-x_tau, one step off the mirror of its own base (reflLink); and the mirrored loop traverses a tau-link backwards, so the reflection carries a dagger there, U_tau(x) -> U_tau(c-1-x_tau)^{-1} (reflConf) -- exactly the dagger in the Osterwalder-Seiler time reflection, and without it the action is not invariant, since the holonomy would come back with the wrong two letters inverted and be conjugate to no plaquette's. Symmetry.onLink is a bare permutation of links and cannot carry a dagger, so wilsonSymmetry is unavailable and the mirrored boundary word turns out to be a cyclic rotation of the image plaquette's own word (bd_reflect_axis_fst/snd) rather than that word -- holonomies equal only up to conjugation (hol_reflConf), which is enough because wilsonDensity is a class function. reflect_action_invariant then runs through Equiv.sum_comp on reflPlaqPerm, and expect_reflect_invariant through LatticeGauge.System.expect_invariant_of_mp, whose measure half needs probHaar invariant under inversion -- Mathlib proves that only for abelian groups, so isInvInvariant_probHaar derives it for a compact one from unimodularity plus Haar uniqueness. Foundation-only. The character expansion is not here and is not attempted.
import MassGap.Transfer  -- The reflection form, its GNS quotient, and a transfer operator on it. reflForm is the Osterwalder-Seiler pairing <(F o theta) G> of the SU(N) Wilson system at the coordinate reflection Reflect.reflConf, symmetric (reflForm_symm, from expect_reflect_invariant and theta^2 = id) and normalised (reflForm_one_one). ReflForm adds the field form_nonneg, which a caller supplies -- no ReflForm is built from the Wilson measure in this file; ReflectionStrong.wilsonGibbsReflForm and LogConvex.wilsonReflForm build one. From that field: Cauchy-Schwarz, the null space as a submodule, and GNS = A / nullSpace as an InnerProductSpace R. TransferData adds a translation T with T_symm, T_contract, T_vac and vac_norm; Tq descends T to the quotient, self-adjoint, fixing the vacuum, of norm at most one, and in finite dimension inner_pow_expand gives <v, T^k v> = sum <e_i, v>^2 lambda_i^k (periodicCorr symmetrises it). one_le_of_eigenvalues_le: a bound r on every eigenvalue forces r >= 1, because the vacuum is a unit eigenvector at 1 -- so a gap is a statement on the vacuum complement. Positivity of T is not a field; eigenvalue_nonneg carries it as a hypothesis. Foundation-only.
import MassGap.HaarMoments      -- strong-coupling workhorse (milestone 1 toward nontrivial clustering): ∫_{SU(2)} U_ij dHaar = 0 (haar_su2_coeff_zero) — fundamental-rep matrix coefficients Haar-average to zero, from left-invariance ∘ the single diagonal element diag(i,-i) (invariant-projection, no Peter–Weyl); the full Schur orthogonality tensor ∫U_ij Ū_kl = ½δ_ik δ_jl (haar_su2_second_moment), character norm ∫|tr U|²=1 (haar_su2_char_norm), and two-point engine ∫tr(UA)tr(U*B)=½tr(AB) (haar_su2_two_point) — all by explicit-element invariance, no Peter–Weyl; foundation-only
import MassGap.WilsonAnalytic -- Differentiability in beta, and the covariance identity. expect_hasDerivAt: d<O>_beta/dbeta = -Cov_beta(O,S) on the genuine Wilson Gibbs measure, by differentiation under the integral (dominated by a constant, the action bounded in [0,4]) plus the quotient rule with Z > 0; wilsonSystem_expect_hasDerivAt repeats it for an arbitrary SU(N) Wilson system, so the plaquette count is explicit. cov_bound_extensive bounds the covariance by 4*M*#Plaq with no other hypothesis; cov_eq_sum_over_plaquettes splits it as a sum over plaquettes, and cov_bound_local / cov_bound_summable replace the plaquette count by the size of a support or the total of a dominating profile, which expect_lipschitz_local / expect_lipschitz_summable carry through the mean-value theorem. The vanishing or domination of the connected plaquette correlator is a hypothesis of that last group and is proved nowhere in this module; WilsonReal.expect_plaqObs_factor makes it hold outright on disjoint plaquettes. Every statement is at a fixed finite lattice. Foundational footprint.
import MassGap.StrongCoupling -- The strong-coupling cluster expansion, on the real Wilson connected correlator. The expansion is an exact finite algebraic identity at every beta (boltz_eq_subset_sum, corrNum_eq_subset_sum) -- no radius, no remainder, no convergence -- and every pair of activated plaquette subsets whose union carries no touching chain from p0 to pd cancels under an explicit exchange across p0's own component (nonbridging_sum_eq_zero), so wilsonCorrConn is a sum over bridging pairs alone over Z^2 (wilsonCorrConn_eq_bridging_sum). card_connSets_le counts the touch-connected cores at each size by ((touchDeg bd + 1)^2)^m -- a connected set is covered by a walk built by repeated detour, two steps per new plaquette -- with Fintype.card Pq in neither statement nor proof; card_rooted_pairs_ge shows that dropping connectedness lets the volume back in, so the hypothesis is load-bearing. hard_core_ratio_le bounds the exponentially small Z^2 on beta >= 0 alone, with no Mayer expansion and no Kotecky-Preiss, because the constrained outside sum is itself a partition function (subset_sum_eq_subPart). coreResummation_holds is the reindexing onto cores, pairTerm_abs_le bounds each term with a derived constant 8, and wilsonCorrConn_abs_le_coreConst_mul_rate_pow assembles them: neither coreConst nor coreRate carries the lattice extent. siteAtHyper_not_mem_ball is the lag-to-ball bridge, with the step constant derived as 1 from bd's own boundary word and shown sharp (touch_advances_one); raw_lag_radius_refuted shows the raw-lag form false on the periodic torus. no_sep_family_of_reachable shows a half-line decay predicate's separation family unsatisfiable once the plaquettes are reachable, and wilsonCorrConn_eq_zero_of_no_ball shows the correlation exactly zero when they are not; the finite-k form wilsonCorrConn_abs_le_coreConst_mul_rate_pow is the one with content. Every statement is at fixed N and fixed beta. Foundational only throughout.
import MassGap.OddLagSplit  -- The odd-lag (link-reflection) action split, and reflection positivity of corrClay at even extent. cfgSum puts gNeg on every reflection-fixed axis link whose site has even coordinate sum, and sum_val_shift_parity is why that works: at even extent a transverse step flips the coordinate-sum parity, so of the two fixed axis links a straddling plaquette reads, exactly one has even sum -- making every straddling plaquette read -1 at once where cfgWitness moves only one. straddling_sum_not_paired_at_odd_lag and no_half_function_for_straddling_sum lift both refutations to the exponential of a sum. oplaq_side partitions every non-degenerate plaquette into one half alone or the straddling class. hsRe_dsum_conj is hsRe_conj for a whole family with each block conjugated by its own gauge pair. cross_word_both_handednesses shows a link reflection has two fixed planes presenting the gauge with opposite handedness; mixedAct_invAt_mul reconciles them by inverting one plane's variables under the U-integral, which needs only invLink_measurePreserving and two locality facts. The block regrouping goes by sumPiEquivProdPi plus one index equivalence (blockEquiv3); the pinned Mathlib has no measurePreserving_piCurry. corrClay_rp_of_odd_lags reduces reflection positivity of corrClay at even extent to PlaqReflPositive at the odd lags (the even lags from ActionSplit.plaqReflPositive_of_even_lag, lag zero from PlaqVariance.corrClay_zero_pos), odd_crossing_integral_nonneg proves the odd-lag integral nonnegative, and corrClay_reflection_positive is the composite, with hypotheses Nap + 1 = 2m, 2 <= m and 0 <= beta and nothing about the lattice; foundational-only. Both restrictions are load-bearing: su3_kernel_nonneg_iff / negctl_odd_discharge_needs_nonneg_coupling show the cross kernel is nonnegative exactly at 0 <= beta, and negctl_plane_assignment_collides_at_m_one shows the backward plane assignment collides at extent two. Odd extents, extent two and negative coupling are outside its domain; ActionSplit.exists_even_extent_aperture supplies an even extent.
import MassGap.CrossingIntegration  -- the odd-lag crossing integration, done with no representation theory -- and the cheap finish refuted first. straddling_word_not_matrix_paired_at_odd_lag and no_half_function_for_straddling_word prove, two independent ways, that the straddling word G F G'-inverse (Theta F)-inverse is not X (Theta X)-dagger and is not any function of the two half-restrictions: a paired form is a square at a reflection-fixed configuration, and cfgWitness -- the identity everywhere except one reflection-FIXED axis link carrying diag(1,-1,-1), which is its own inverse and is why the configuration is fixed -- makes the straddling word read Re tr = -1. The second obstruction is stronger: a fixed axis link lies in neither half, so moving it moves the word and leaves both half-restrictions untouched, which refutes every candidate including rescalings and additive constants. The positive result: the plane links act on the transverse links exactly as a gauge field acts on link variables, so the straddling word is the same cross form the Wilson weight already has with the plane links absorbed into one argument. hsRe_conj says the cross form does not see the plane gauge (cyclicity of the trace), wilson_kernel_integral_psd says the weight is a positive-semidefinite kernel against any probability measure, and crossing_gauge_integral_eq moves the plane average onto both factors of the pairing -- which is the job Schur orthogonality does in Osterwalder-Seiler, done without it. The moment route was checked and is the expensive one: exp(beta Re tr W)'s order-k term is degree k in the plane-link coordinates, so it needs balanced moments at every order, and HaarMoments is exact for SU(2) only through order 3 and brackets the balanced fourth in [1/4, 1/2]; truncating at the second moment is 6.5 percent off at beta = 1 and is a strong-coupling expansion of the weight, not the weight. Scope, stated because it was overreached in a first draft: both obstructions are about one straddling plaquette, and the Boltzmann weight's straddling factor is the exponential of a sum -- the sum-level claim is not proved. And wilson_crossing_pairing_nonneg is over an abstract Omega, Gamma, X; nothing ties X to wilsonHol. What remains for odd lags is the action split, lattice bookkeeping of the kind ActionSplit does for even lags, with no representation theory in it.
import MassGap.CharacterExpansion  -- the SU(N) Wilson weight is positive-definite at physical coupling, and two mechanism gaps closed. wilson_kernel_nonneg: exp(beta * Re tr(A B-dagger)) is a positive-definite kernel for beta >= 0, proved with no representation theory -- Re tr(A B-dagger) is the Euclidean inner product on the 2N^2 real coordinates, so every power splits into paired monomials with no cross terms and the coefficients are multiplicities written out as a monomial index. Negative control as an iff: two genuine SU(3) elements and weights (1,-1) give 2(exp(3 beta) - exp(-beta)), nonnegative exactly when 0 <= beta, so the hypothesis is load-bearing and is seen to block. Not formalised, and the docstring says so: the Peter-Weyl dictionary. Nothing here uses it, so irreducible characters are never named -- do not read these as SU(3) character theory in Lean. Two gaps closed: twisted_reflection_positive_of_expansion points the tree's expansion mechanism at the actual reflection (reflection_positive_of_expansion is stated for a bare relabelling U-compose-theta, while reflConf daggers the axis links, so it was inapplicable), and crossweight_pairing_nonneg is the first statement in the tree admitting a term that genuinely couples the two halves, where ActionSplit's weld requires every plaquette to read one side plus the plane. The blocker is not the coefficients: odd_lag_straddling_plaq_two_fixed_axis_links proves the straddling plaquette word carries two distinct fixed axis links, both inverted by the reflection, so the word is G F G'-inverse (Theta F)-inverse and there is no paired word to expand -- the expansion has nothing to act on. What remains is the crossing-link Haar integration yielding a Gram matrix, i.e. Schur orthogonality / Peter-Weyl, which Mathlib does not supply for SU(3).
import MassGap.ActionSplit  -- the three-block weld: the shared block integrated outside the square. ReflectionPositivity.pairing_with_reflection_nonneg needs Disjoint S T, but under a reflection the links on the plane are read by both halves, and WilsonReal.block_integral_factor is two-block plus disjoint. pairing_eq_weighted_square supplies the conditional form: the integral of W(U|R) * O * O(twist U) equals the integral of W(U|R) * (half-integral)^2, with the shared block outside the square, the OS dagger carried by twist (reflConf_eq_twist), and a nonnegative plane weight riding along -- which is where exp(-beta S0) goes; wilson_pairing_nonneg_of_shared_block states it on the Wilson lattice. Negative control: on a fair coin the true pairing is 1/2 while the inside-the-square form gives 1/4, and shared_block_inside_square_false refutes the inside form outright. The reflection's parity structure: at even extent with even constant the fixed set is transverse links only (no_fixed_axis_link) and is nonempty (exists_fixed_transverse_link); at odd constant, and at every constant when the extent is odd, a fixed axis link exists and reflConf inverts it (reflConf_inverts_fixed_axis_link). plaqReflPositive_of_even_lag gives PlaqReflPositive at every even lag; the odd lags are OddLagSplit's. exists_even_extent_aperture supplies an even extent. Foundational only.
import MassGap.GibbsPositive  -- Strict positivity of the Gibbs expectation of the plaquette energy, at every coupling. corrNum_plaqObs_pos proves 0 < corrNum from continuity plus one configuration: probHaar is Haar hence IsOpenPosMeasure, Measure.pi carries that to configurations, so a continuous nonnegative integrand integrating to zero vanishes at every configuration; 0 < partition is WilsonReal.wilsonSystem_partition_pos, reused. The gauge input is one group element: gD = diag(1,-1,-1), whose Wilson density is 4/3 (wilsonDensity_gD). Negative controls: wilsonSystem_expect_sub_const gives the expectation of a shifted observable, and expect_plaqObs_su_one_eq_zero gives exactly 0 at SU(1), so the group content is load-bearing. Each statement is at a fixed coupling; nothing here bounds the expectation from below uniformly in beta.
import MassGap.PlaqVariance  -- Strict positivity of the plaquette variance. corrClay_zero_pos proves 0 < corrClay (N+1) beta 0 for all N and all real beta, including zero and negative, foundational-only. It is one of the two inputs ReflectPositive.corrClay_rp_of consumes and is not reflection positivity: the identically-zero correlation satisfies every pairing inequality and fails it. At lag 0 the two plaquettes are the same plaquette (siteAtHyper tau 0 = fun _ => 0), so no displacement or translation invariance is needed. The mechanism is wilsonCorrConn_self_pos, stated for any SU(N) and any boundary word: product Haar over links is IsOpenPosMeasure, so Continuous.ae_eq_iff_eq turns a continuous nonnegative integrand integrating to zero into vanishing at every configuration, which with the strictly positive Boltzmann weight forces the observable globally constant. The gauge-group input is one inequality: confOne gives phi = 0, confAB gives phi = 4/3 via a commutator of two real involutions in SU(3). Negative control: at SU(1) su_one_has_no_two_values makes the hypothesis unsatisfiable and wilsonCorrConn_self_eq_zero_of_trivial gives exactly 0, so the hypothesis carries the whole gauge-group content.
import MassGap.ReflectPositive  -- The connected lag correlation written as a reflection pairing, and its nonnegativity reduced to one hypothesis. corrHyper_eq_pairing proves the lag displacement is the tree's reflection: corrHyper equals the Gibbs pairing of the centred plaquette-energy observable with its own reflConf image, via hol_reflConf and expect_reflect_invariant (EW_plaqE_lag: the one-point function does not move). PlaqReflPositive is that pairing's nonnegativity for every centring constant, the Osterwalder-Seiler inequality narrowed to one observable, with its locality side condition machine-checked (plaq_link_ne_refl: every link of the base plaquette sits at lag-coordinate 0 and its mirror at c, so the two halves read disjoint links at nonzero lag). corrHyper_nonneg_of_reflPositive turns it into 0 <= corrHyper at each lag, and corrClay_rp_of assembles nonnegativity at every lag with 0 < corrClay ... 0, which is the plaquette variance and not reflection positivity -- the identically-zero correlation satisfies every pairing inequality and fails it. pairing_nonpos_of_odd: oddObs has pairing -<O^2> <= 0, so reflection positivity cannot be stated observable-free. OddLagSplit.corrClay_reflection_positive discharges the hypothesis at even extent, 2 <= m, 0 <= beta.
import MassGap.LogConvex  -- Log-convexity of the lag correlation in the lag, constant one, the first coupling-free ratio inequality in the tree: corrClay (e1 + e2)^2 <= corrClay (e1 + e1) * corrClay (e2 + e2) at every even extent, every real coupling and every pair of lags strictly below half the extent. Reflection positivity is a statement about a bilinear form and the tree had only its diagonal; ActionSplit.pairing_nonneg_of_local was already quantified over observables, so the diagonal holds on a whole vector space -- localObs, the bounded measurable functions reading one half-space plus the plane -- and wilsonReflForm is the first Transfer.ReflForm ever built from the Wilson measure, every field proved: symmetry from the measure-preserving involution plus a plane weight the reflection does not move, bilinearity from the integral, form_nonneg from the three-block weld. Transfer.ReflForm.cauchy_schwarz then reads the off-diagonal against the two diagonals. The constant is one because the geometry makes it one: all three pairings come from one reflection at one plane and share one partition function, so nothing is left over to be a constant. integrand_eq_paired_pair is ActionSplit.integrand_eq_paired with the two plaquettes kept apart -- its proof never needed them equal, the mirror identity actPlus o Theta = actMinus is about the action. EW_plaqE_pair_shift is translation invariance derived from the reflection (<phi_P phi_Q> = <phi_0 phi_{P-Q}>, one application of expect_reflect_invariant at constant P, both plaquettes moving under the same map), so no separate translation symmetry was needed. Two hypotheses the odd-lag work needed are absent here. 0 <= beta is not required: this runs entirely on the even-lag weld, a conditional square against a strictly positive Boltzmann weight that never reads the coupling's sign. The odd-lag link reflection is not required either, though the cross lag e1 + e2 may be odd -- an odd lag reached as an off-diagonal pairing under an even reflection fixes no axis link, so there is no dagger to reconcile. What is required is the lags below m = half the extent: that is the reflection geometry's own bound, the two fixed planes are m apart and both levels must sit strictly between them. Non-vacuity at extent four: corrClay_log_convex_at_extent_four gives rho(1)^2 <= rho(0) * rho(2), a genuine three-lag statement; the diagonal case is an equality, which is the check that no constant crept in. Foundational only.
import MassGap.ContactFloor  -- An aperture-uniform floor on the contact term. wilsonCorrConn_self_ge_haar holds at every geometry and every SU(Nc): Var_beta(phi_p) >= exp(-2*beta*card(touchNbrs bd p)) * Var_0(phi_p), where the exponent counts the plaquettes sharing a link with p and nothing else. The action splits as S_touch + S_rest because a plaquette that does not touch p reads none of p's links (ReflectionPositivity.action_on_congr_of_support); e^{-beta*S_touch} lies in [e^{-2*beta*K}, 1] since each density is in [0,2]; the centred square and e^{-beta*S_rest} read disjoint link blocks so block_integral_factor factors them and the common integral cancels against the partition function; and haar_centred_le discards the Gibbs mean, which carries both the coupling and the aperture, for the Haar mean, which carries neither. StrongCoupling.touchDeg_bd_le caps the exponent at 16*dim with no extent in it -- 64 at dim 4, so e^{-128*beta}. integral_hol_clay closes the pure-Haar variance corrClay n 0 0: at extent n >= 2 the four links of the plaquette are distinct, so left-translating the last one identifies the law of the holonomy as Haar itself, making corrClay n 0 0 the same number at every n >= 2; extent one is handled as a single lattice. The floor is therefore the min of two extent-free numbers times e^{-128*b} on each coupling interval [0, b], with positivity from PlaqVariance.corrClay_zero_pos. It degrades as b grows: a floor on each bounded coupling interval, not one number for the half-line. Foundational only.
import MassGap.HaarVariance  -- The pure-Haar plaquette variance, a universal constant with no lattice extent and no coupling in it: haar_variance_reTr_pos gives 0 < Var(Re tr U) under normalized Haar on SU(N) at every N >= 2, and nothing in the statement or the proof mentions a volume, a boundary condition or a beta. The mechanism is PlaqVariance's, run against bare Haar: Re tr is continuous, probHaar is a Haar measure hence IsOpenPosMeasure, so Continuous.ae_eq_iff_eq turns a continuous nonnegative centred square integrating to zero into vanishing at every group element. The gauge-group input is two exhibited elements: the identity reads N, and flipEl = diag(-1,-1,1,...,1) -- determinant 1, a genuine SU(N) element -- reads N-4, which is where N >= 2 is consumed. The four-link form is reduced, not assumed: measurePreserving_prod4 proves the ordered product of four independent Haar elements is itself Haar-distributed, using only left-invariance, and variance_comp_measurePreserving carries the variance across, so four_link_variance_eq makes the plaquette variance equal to the one-element variance. The positivity is existential: it gives non-vanishing, not a lower bound on the value. Negative control: haar_variance_reTr_su_one gives exactly 0 at SU(1), so 2 <= N is load-bearing. Foundational only.
import MassGap.ReflectionStrong  -- Clay row A4 -- reflection positivity at every real coupling, on the even lags. wilson_expect_nonneg_module: 0 <= <F.(F o Theta)> on the genuine Wilson Gibbs state for every F in the module of bounded measurable slab observables, with beta free. wilson_expect_gram_nonneg is the PSD Gram matrix over a finite family, and mul_mem_localObs makes that module a unital subalgebra -- positive semidefiniteness over an algebra, not one observable. Carried to the Clay object by corrClay_nonneg_even_lag at every real beta, with corrClay_nonneg_even_lag_at_negative_coupling witnessing it at beta = -1. Why the sign condition survives on the odd lags: OddLagSplit.corrClay_reflection_positive is a composite of three sources -- even lags from the site reflection, where wPlane factors out as a strictly positive weight and a conditional square remains so nothing reads beta's sign; odd lags from the link reflection, whose straddling factor is the Wilson cross kernel; and the positive-mass conjunct from PlaqVariance.corrClay_zero_pos, which is not reflection positivity at all. su3_kernel_nonneg_iff is an iff about that cross kernel only, so it governs the odd-lag route and is silent on the even-lag one -- the two are different objects. wilsonGibbsReflForm is a Transfer.ReflForm on Transfer.reflForm itself with vac_norm discharged.
import MassGap.WilsonTransfer  -- The lattice time shift as an operator on Wilson observables, and the slab obstruction as a theorem. shiftConf_eq_reflConf_comp is the fact the file runs on: one lattice step is the composite of the two site reflections whose constants differ by one, the axis dagger appearing twice and cancelling, so the shift needs no new measure theory. From it: expect_shift_invariant -- the Gibbs expectation is invariant under one lattice step, for an arbitrary observable. shiftObs is the operator, an algebra map on the full observable space (shiftObs_one, shiftObs_mul). reflForm_shiftObs_symm proves <T F, G> = <F, T G> for the Gibbs reflection form at every real beta and every reflection constant, with no measurability, boundedness or positivity hypothesis; shiftObs_one gives T_vac definitionally. They do not assemble into a TransferData, and shift_not_stable_on_slab says why: an axis link at level m-1 sits in blkS u blkR and one step along tau puts it at level m, which the union refuses. A slab of width m is not a half-line and the shift is what tells them apart.
import MassGap.HalfLineTransfer  -- The lattice shift as a transfer operator on a periodic lattice. shiftObs_pow_period: (shiftObs tau)^n F = F for every observable, no hypothesis on the module, the coupling or the reflection -- n steps along tau is the identity on sites. const_of_shift_stable: at even extent n = 2m with 2 <= m, every shift-stable submodule of the slab algebra consists of constants, and shiftObs_eq_self_of_shift_stable makes the shift the identity on it. The argument quantifies over submodules, not support sets: determination is closed under intersection (detBy_inter, proved by splicing two configurations along the set), stability pushes it through every power of the shift (detBy_iterate), and orbitCore_eq_empty says no link keeps its whole tau-orbit inside the slab. blkR_shift_stable_of_extent_two shows 2 <= m is sharp -- at n = 2 the transverse levels are already the whole cycle. TransferGap.finite_order_fails_gap is the gap-side consequence of finite order.
import MassGap.InfiniteLattice  -- The infinite four-dimensional lattice, and why the thermodynamic limit exists. ISite = Fin 4 -> Z, so the lattice is genuinely infinite, and compactSpace_IConf is the load-bearing fact: IConf G = ILink -> G is a product of compact groups, so CompactSpace fires by Tychonoff and needs neither countability of ILink nor a named topology -- inferInstance closes it. That is why infinite-volume Gibbs states exist at all for a compact gauge group. Countability is spent elsewhere, on the Borel structure: borelSpace_IConf and secondCountable_IConf are the only consumers of Countable ILink and both fail without it, so a Borel measure on IConf G is a theorem with hypotheses rather than free. Compactness also removes two clauses LogConvex.localObs has to carry: bounded_of_continuous and measurable_of_continuous derive boundedness and measurability, so localAlg asks only for continuity plus locality. Continuity rather than measurability is the deliberate choice in the carrier, because a function of finitely many coordinates need not be continuous and a weak-* limit of states is tested against continuous functions. ibd mirrors WilsonHypercubic.bd with Z in place of Fin n, and wilsonHol_periodic states the direction that holds: the finite theory is the periodic sector of the infinite one -- a finite configuration pulls back and its holonomies agree on the nose. There is no map the other way; a periodisation would have to choose a fundamental domain and the choice would not commute with ibd across the boundary.
import MassGap.InfiniteShift  -- A time translation that is neither the identity nor of finite order -- the first carrier in the tree on which the shift is a non-trivial endomorphism. Transfer.TransferData asks for T : A -> A, an endomorphism, and the tree closes every finite carrier twice over: HalfLineTransfer.shiftObs_eq_self_of_shift_stable makes the shift the identity on the fixed-block slab algebra, and TransferGap.finite_order_fails_gap kills any algebra on the torus because shiftObs_pow_period gives shiftObs^[n] = id. Read the first no-go's hypothesis: const_of_shift_stable requires every element of M to lie in localObs (blkS) (blkR) -- determined by one fixed finite block -- because detBy_orbitCore intersects the shifted copies of that block and orbitCore_eq_empty empties the intersection. A directed union satisfies no such hypothesis: an observable in it is determined by some finite set and the shift carries that set to another finite set, so the support moves rather than stays. InfiniteLattice.quasiLocalAlg is that union and ISite = Fin 4 -> Z makes it infinite. Proved here: ishiftObs_mem_quasiLocalAlg (the shift is an endomorphism, support moving from S to S.image ishiftLink), ishiftObs_ne_id_of_separating (not the identity), and ishiftObs_infinite_order (no positive power is the identity, so finite_order_fails_gap has no p to apply at) -- the latter resting on ishift_iterate, that k steps add k to a coordinate, which is where Z rather than Fin n is doing the work. Not proved, and it is the larger half: no reflection form, no positivity, no TransferData. Those need the infinite-volume state (DLRLimit) with reflection positivity transported to it. This is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one: a carrier on which the shift is trivial is certainly useless, but non-triviality alone does not build the transfer operator.
import MassGap.LatticeReflection  -- The reflection of Z^4, as an InfiniteReflection.Reflection. Sites reflect by x_tau -> c - x_tau (ireflSite), and links by the base shift ireflLink tau c (mu,x) = (mu, if mu = tau then ireflSite tau (c-1) x else ireflSite tau c x), because a tau-link spans [x_tau, x_tau+1] so its mirror spans [c-1-x_tau, c-x_tau] and is based at the lower end. ireflConf additionally inverts the group element on every tau-link -- the dagger, since the mirrored loop traverses such a link backwards -- and ireflPlaq transposes the plane when it contains tau. Involutivity at each level (ireflSite_involutive, ireflLink_involutive, ireflPlaq_involutive, ireflConf_involutive), the commutation identities with ishift, and ihol_ireflConf: the holonomy in the reflected configuration is conjugate to the holonomy of the image plaquette. latticeReflection packages it with all four fields proved. Reflection positivity of a state for this reflection is a hypothesis of the consumers (ReflPositiveOn), discharged at the periodic state in PeriodicState.
import MassGap.ReflectionShift  -- The reflection conjugates the time shift, on Z^4 -- three of ShiftCompat's four fields, settled. T_mul is ishiftObsL_mul (precomposition is multiplicative), T_S is ishiftObsL_iunshiftObs (the backward shift is a right inverse), and THETA_T is ireflObs_ishiftObs: theta(T f) = S(theta f), the reflection carrying the forward shift to the backward one. It is a transcription, not a new idea: WilsonTransfer.shiftConf_reflConf_shiftConf proves the same identity on the finite periodic lattice and the tree already spends it on reflForm_shiftObs_symm, the finite-volume T_symm -- the same role theta_T plays in SchwarzIteration.form_shift_symm. What was missing is the version on InfiniteLattice's types: LatticeReflection never mentions ishift, InfiniteShift never mentions a reflection, and no backward shift existed on IConf at all (GibbsSpec has an iunshift on sites but is an import-isolated duplicate of the skeleton, so nothing on this side can reach it). The geometry: over Z exactly as over Fin n, a step along the axis moves the reflection constant by one -- ireflSite tau c (ishift tau x) = ireflSite tau (c-1) x, because c - (x+1) = (c-1) - x -- and on links the base shift of ireflLink travels with it, so ireflLink tau c . ishiftLink tau = ireflLink tau (c-1) covers both cases of the direction split. The dagger never has to be tracked: ishiftLink and iunshiftLink preserve a link's direction, so both sides carry the same l.1 = tau split and the inverses cancel case by case. Not closed: nu_T, that the state does not see a translation -- a property of the measure, not of these maps -- so ShiftCompat is still not constructible. What this removes is the part that is about the lattice.
import MassGap.HalfSpaceAlgebra  -- The positive half-space algebra, and the shift preserves it -- the Osterwalder-Seiler setup, which no finite carrier in the tree could supply. InfiniteReflection takes the half-space algebra as a parameter it does not construct; this constructs it. Why the slab could not: const_of_shift_stable says a shift-stable submodule of the fixed-block slab algebra is constants, because orbitCore_eq_empty empties the intersection of one bounded block's shifted copies. A half-space is not a bounded block: shifting {x_tau >= c} in the +tau direction gives {x_tau >= c+1}, a subset (shift_strictly_shrinks_support, posHalf_succ_subset), so the region moves into itself rather than off itself and nothing forces constancy. halfSpaceAlg_shift_stable is the endomorphism statement; with InfiniteShift.ishiftObs_infinite_order it gives a subalgebra the time translation maps into itself on which that translation is neither the identity nor of finite order -- the slab fails stability without triviality, the torus fails infinite order. Also: one_mem_halfSpaceAlg (so the vacuum exists and stateReflForm_vac_norm applies), halfLinkObs_mem (it is not the constants) and shift_moves_halfLinkObs (the shift is not the identity on this submodule, which a submodule could in principle have made false). Not proved: no state. Nothing exhibits a reflection-positive reflection-invariant state on IConf G, and TransferData's T_symm (the reflection must conjugate the shift to its inverse) and T_contract (the Schwarz iteration) are both still open.
import MassGap.SchwarzIteration  -- T_CONTRACT is DERIVED from T_SYMM, not assumed beside it. TransferData has six fields; two concern the translation's relation to the form. T_SYMM: form_shift_symm proves nu(theta(Tf).g) = nu(theta f.Tg) from ShiftCompat in four rewrites -- the reflection conjugates the forward shift to the backward one, the state is translation-invariant, a translation is multiplicative, and translating back then forward is nothing. That is where reflection positivity earns its keep. T_CONTRACT: contract_of_bounded_orbit derives it. Moving one T across the form and applying Cauchy-Schwarz gives a(n+1)^2 <= a(n) a(n+2) -- midpoint log-convexity of the orbit diagonal -- so if a1 ever exceeded a0 the ratios would be non-decreasing and a(n) would grow geometrically, which a bounded orbit forbids. Boundedness is genuinely needed: on A = R with form x y = x y and T x = 2x the form is symmetric and PSD and <Tx,Tx> = 4x^2 > x^2, so 'from symmetry alone' would be false. orbit_bounded_of_state supplies it -- a state is bounded by the sup norm, the sup norm is submultiplicative, and neither reflection nor translation increases it. What is consumed, not eliminated: T_symm itself, as the hypothesis hsym of orbit_log_convex, contract_of_bounded_orbit and null_is_preserved. And ShiftCompat forces the translation to be invertible (TransferAssembly.shift_inverse_is_two_sided) -- satisfiable on Z, not on a half-line of N. Nothing here delivers TransferData.T_symm directly; that needs a carrier, which is TransferAssembly's work.
import MassGap.TransferAssembly  -- A full TransferData, every field discharged, from a state and a reflection. assembleTransferData takes a Reflection, an infinite-volume State, the half-space Submodule, reflection-invariance, reflection-POSITIVITY on that submodule, a ShiftCompat, forward-stability of the submodule (HalfSpaceAlgebra.halfSpaceAlg_shift_stable), the constant being in it and fixed by the shift, and two sup-norm bounds -- and produces a genuine Transfer.TransferData. T_symm and T_contract are not among the inputs: they are proved, by form_shift_symm and contract_of_bounded_orbit fed by orbit_bounded_of_state. restrictT and restrictT_iterate are the carrier bridge, restricting the shift to the submodule and showing the restriction's iterate is the iterate's restriction, which is what lets an orbit bound stated on C(X,R) be read on the submodule. shift_inverse_is_two_sided proves ShiftCompat's hidden consequence: T_S plus theta_T plus involutivity give S . T = id, so the translation is invertible on the full algebra -- consistent with the half-space algebra being only FORWARD-stable, and consistent only because the lattice is infinite. The one input the tree cannot supply is hpos: reflection positivity of an infinite-volume Wilson state on the half-space algebra. Everything else on the list is built or is a routine property of precomposition. So this is a reduction of the Yang-Mills transfer operator to one hypothesis, with the rest machine-checked -- not a construction, and it does not prove the operator exists.
import MassGap.WilsonTransferReduction  -- The transfer operator, reduced to three facts about the state. TransferAssembly.assembleTransferData takes nine inputs; six are about the lattice and the maps and all six are discharged here. ShiftCompat comes from nu_T alone (shiftCompat_of_nu_T -- the other three fields are ReflectionShift's: ishiftObsL_mul is T_mul, ishiftObsL_iunshiftObs is T_S, ireflObs_ishiftObs is theta_T). hstable is halfSpaceAlg_shift_stable, hone is one_mem_halfSpaceAlg, hTone is ishiftObsL_one, and the two norm bounds are norm_comp_le -- precomposition cannot enlarge a supremum. What remains is exactly three, all about the measure: IsReflectionInvariant and ReflPositiveOn on the half-space algebra at the constant 2p, and translation invariance of the state. transferData_of_state_facts is the statement. positiveTransfer_iff_odd_reflPositive reads PositiveTransfer as reflection positivity at the odd constant 2p-1, and gapAt_iff_pairing / gapAt_iff_subtracted_pairing restate GapAt as a pairing inequality. PeriodicState supplies a state with all four facts at every beta >= 0.
import MassGap.WilsonState  -- A probability measure as a DLRLimit.State. stateOfMeasure is integration against P, with the four State fields from integral_add, integral_const_mul, integral_nonneg and measure_univ, and integrability from boundedness on a compact space (integrable_of_continuousMap). exists_wilson_infinite_volume_state applies it to the measure WilsonDLR produces, carrying the DLR equation through. wilsonTransferData composes stateOfMeasure into WilsonTransferReduction.transferData_of_state_facts, which takes three facts about the state as hypotheses (reflection invariance and reflection positivity at the constant 2c, shift invariance), and checks by elaboration that GibbsSpec's and InfiniteLattice's copies of ISite/ILink/IConf are defeq. At zero coupling: refl_pairing_at_zero_eq_zero, gapAt_zero_at_zero_coupling, hnu_at_zero.
import MassGap.GibbsSpec  -- DLR consistency of the Wilson specification on the infinite lattice. dlr_consistent: for every Lambda subset Lambda', every measurable f with |f| <= 1 and every boundary configuration omega, gamma_Lambda' composed with gamma_Lambda equals gamma_Lambda'. The density hypotheses are only Measurable, 0 <= phi and phi <= 2, and nothing about the group beyond Group, MeasurableSpace, MeasurableMul2, MeasurableInv and a probability measure. wilson_dlr_consistent instantiates it at WilsonAction.wilsonDensity on SU(N) against CompactGauge.probHaar at every real beta. The load-bearing lemma is actionBC_split: the boundary action over Lambda' splits as the Lambda part plus a remainder evaluated at the outside configuration alone, resting on links_not_mem_of_mem_sdiff. plaqsIn_split_not_local exhibits nested volumes for which the split's locality hypothesis fails for the free-boundary set plaqsIn, which is why boundaryPlaqs is used; boundaryPlaqs_nonempty rules out an empty energy. IsGibbsMeasure, ExistsGibbsMeasure and TranslationCovariant are Prop-valued definitions with no theorem in this module.
import MassGap.DLRLimit  -- The infinite-volume Gibbs state exists. exists_infinite_volume_gibbs_measure: given a compact, T2, second-countable gauge group, a specification and consistent finite-volume states, there is a probability measure on IConf G whose state satisfies DLR at every finite volume. The economy that makes it cheap: on a compact space, positivity plus normalisation alone give |nu f| <= ||f|| (State.abs_le_norm, via State.mono and State.le_norm) with no continuity or boundedness hypothesis, so each coordinate lands in a compact interval fixed by f alone and the compactness needed is one Icc per observable -- Banach-Alaoglu's proof specialised to the case where the bound is free, with none of the normed-dual API. exists_limit_state works for any NeBot filter on any index type with no directedness, countability or metrisability; the four state fields transport by tendsto_nhds_unique and ge_of_tendsto. IsDLR is written on observables, which is where it is a closed condition, so isDLR_of_tendsto is inheritance rather than construction. borelSpace_iconf is where countability of ILink is spent: over an uncountable index the product sigma-algebra is strictly smaller than the Borel one and Gibbs measure would name a different object. Non-triviality is sized rather than claimed: nu 1 = 1 is a field and rules out the zero functional and nothing else; point-mass exclusion is proved conditionally on a variance floor holding uniformly in the volume, and GibbsPositive.corrNum_plaqObs_pos is not that floor -- it bounds a correlation at a fixed volume and a correlation is not a variance. gamma carries the Feller property in its type (continuous functions to continuous functions), which GibbsSpec proves measurability but not continuity of -- put there deliberately so it cannot be skipped silently.
import MassGap.InfiniteReflection  -- Reflection positivity on a submodule, and its transport to a limit. A TransferData needs form_nonneg, which is reflection positivity. reflection_positivity_fails_off_the_half_space proves that 0 <= nu(theta F . F) is false for all observables -- whenever theta F = -F it reads -nu(F.F) < 0 -- so the condition is asserted on a submodule (ReflPositiveOn); ReflectionStrong.wilsonGibbsReflForm is carried on localObs (blkS) (blkR) for the same reason. The transport: DLRLimit.le_of_eventually_le passes a uniform lower bound to the limit, so reflPositive_of_tendsto is that lemma once per observable and isReflectionInvariant_of_tendsto is uniqueness of limits; pairing_le_of_eventually_pointwise and connected_pairing_le_of_eventually transport pairing inequalities. stateReflForm builds a genuine Transfer.ReflForm on the submodule from four properties of the reflection plus invariance and positivity, and stateReflForm_vac_norm settles vac_norm when the constant is in the submodule. Bracketed both ways: trivialReflection_positive_always shows theta = id makes the condition empty, and the failure theorem shows dropping the submodule makes it false.
import MassGap.OSPositivity  -- Clay row A4 -- OS positivity: sesquilinearity and the algebra clause.Sesquilinearity: Mathlib has no complexification of a real PSD bilinear form, so cform is built on Transfer.PreForm with conjugate symmetry, conjugate-linear left and linear right, real diagonal and the complex Gram cform_gram_re_nonneg / cform_gram_im_zero at complex coefficients and complex test vectors. It is not an analogue: cform_eq_osPairing proves cform at the pair of parts of F, G is the Gibbs expectation of conj(F o Theta) . G componentwise, which needed EW additivity discharged from localObs's own bound. So wilson_osC_diag_re_nonneg gives 0 <= <conj(F o Theta) . F> at beta free and wilson_osC_diag_im_zero gives that the pairing is real -- together OS3's inequality in full. The algebra clause: prod_mem_localObs extends to arbitrary finite products and wilson_gram_nonneg_monomials is the combination -- the Gram matrix over the monomials in any family of slab generators is PSD, i.e. positive semidefiniteness over the generated unital subalgebra rather than over a subspace. What still separates the tree from OS3 is the slab/half-space geometry, the limits, the link reflections and the other OS axioms. Free bonus: Transfer.GNS at wilsonGibbsReflForm is already a genuine InnerProductSpace R -- slab_inner_mk and slab_vac_norm give the Hilbert space and a unit vacuum at finite volume on the slab, today. wilsonSlabTransfer builds a complete TransferData from exactly two named Props, SlabShiftStable and SlabShiftContractive, every other field being a theorem at every real beta -- and SlabShiftStable is very likely false, since HalfLineTransfer.const_of_shift_stable makes any submodule satisfying it consist of constants.
import MassGap.WilsonDLR  -- An infinite-volume Gibbs measure for the Wilson theory. exists_wilson_infinite_volume_gibbs_measure: for every N != 0 and every real beta there is a probability measure P on IConf (SU N) with the integral of spec wilsonDensity beta Lambda probHaar f against P equal to the integral of f, at every finite volume and every continuous f. No coupling regime, no volume bound, no smallness condition; the plaquette set is boundaryPlaqs so the conditioning is a genuine boundary condition. The Feller property: DLRLimit's gamma demands continuous to continuous and GibbsSpec proves measurability; continuous_spec_right gets continuity from MeasureTheory.continuous_of_dominated, the domination by a constant because IConf G is compact, and GibbsSpec.part_pos keeping the quotient continuous. isLocalOn_spec and spec_mem_localAlg give the locality half. GibbsSpec.DLRConsistent quantifies over |f| <= 1 while DLRLimit's hcons quantifies over all continuous f; spec_smul rescales and dlr_general is the result. At zero coupling the module proves uniqueness of the DLR state (dlr_unique_at_zero) and its permutation and twist invariance. The DLR equation holds against continuous observables, not all bounded measurable ones, so GibbsSpec.IsGibbsMeasure is not claimed; uniqueness at positive coupling and translation invariance are not proved, and the limit is along an ultrafilter refining atTop.
import MassGap.SliceTrace  -- Regrouping the link measure along the slab partition. measurePreserving_confEquiv: for any map c : iota -> kappa out of a finite index type, the product measure over iota is the image of the product over kappa of the product measures over the fibres, through a measurable equivalence that moves integrals both ways. The pinned Mathlib (v4.31.0) has MeasurableEquiv.piCurry but no measure-preserving currying for Measure.pi; measurePreserving_sigmaUncurry proves it from Measure.pi_eq on rectangles plus Finset.prod_sigma. slabSupport is the links at times t and t+1; slabSupport_eq_univ_iff proves it is every link exactly when n <= 2, so it is a proper subset from n >= 3 (slabSupport_ne_univ). The Wilson weight on the link type: latticeBoltz, partition_eq_integral_latticeBoltz, corrNum_eq_integral, latticeExpect; prod_split_at splits a product over Fin n at a cut. No kernel is defined, no cyclic integral is formed, no spectrum appears and no gauge fixing is used.
import MassGap.SecondEigenvalue  -- An operator bound on an invariant subspace from a Rayleigh bound, with no finite dimension. norm_le_of_rayleigh_le is one Cauchy-Schwarz on the semidefinite form B(u,v) = <Tu,v> read at two vectors: ||Ty||^4 = B(y,Ty)^2 <= B(y,y) B(Ty,Ty) <= Lambda||y||^2 . Lambda||Ty||^2. No compactness, no completeness, no eigenvalue ordering, no min-max, no trace class, no Perron-Frobenius and no spectral theorem. norm_le_of_iterate_bound and norm_le_of_absolute_iterate_bound turn iterate bounds into one-step bounds. lambdaTwo is an sSup of Rayleigh quotients with both directions of the variational characterisation (lambdaTwo_le_of_rayleigh_le, rayleigh_le_of_lambdaTwo_le, norm_le_lambdaTwo_mul), Nonempty and BddAbove carried where the sSup would otherwise be junk; lambdaTwo_le_of_span_decay and lambdaTwo_le_of_iterate_bound bound it from decay. vacPerp is the vacuum complement and vacPerp_eq_orthogonal proves it is Mathlib's (span R {Omega})^perp.
import MassGap.LinkGram  -- Link-reflection positivity for a general half-local observable. OddLagSplit.odd_crossing_integral_nonneg proves the crossing integral nonnegative for one plaquette-energy observable, and only three steps of that chain look at the observable -- half-locality, measurability and boundedness -- while CrossingIntegration.wilson_crossing_pairing_nonneg, which the chain ends in, already takes an arbitrary bounded measurable function of the half's variables. gram_refl_positive re-runs the chain with the observable as a parameter: 0 <= EW N beta (F * F o reflConf tau (a+a+1)) for every measurable bounded F reading oblkS tau a m alone, at N != 0, even extent n = 2m with 2 <= m, and 0 <= beta. No GNS space and no TransferData are used.
import MassGap.GNSHilbert  -- A complete complex Hilbert space and a unit vacuum from a reflection form. Pre P is the pair module A x A as a complex vector space with the semi-inner product built from OSPositivity.cform; H P is its UniformSpace.Completion, complex and complete (complete_H), the completion doing the null-space quotient. Omega is the vacuum (norm_Omega, nontrivial_H), cT / opT the complexified transfer step, a self-adjoint contraction fixing Omega (norm_opT_le_one, isSelfAdjoint_opT, opT_Omega), with 1 the greatest element of its spectrum (isGreatest_one_spectrum_opT). PositiveTransfer is operator positivity, giving isPositive_opT and spectrum_opT_nonneg; positiveTransfer_of_gram supplies it from a Gram condition. At the Wilson Gibbs reflection form: ymH, ymOmega_norm (needs only N /= 0 and n = 2m, 0 < m), nontrivial_ymH, and ym_inner_eq_osPairing -- the inner product is one Gibbs expectation EW N beta (conj(F . Theta) * G) at every real beta. The trivial target is recorded as a theorem: trivialTransfer and ym_target_discharged_trivially discharge every clause with T the identity, and shiftSlab_eq_id / shift_descends_to_id show the slab shift is the identity on OSPositivity.SlabShiftStable's premise, so a transfer that moves something is the content.
import MassGap.GNSCompare  -- Relating the two Hilbert spaces built from one TransferData: the real quotient Transfer.GNS = A / nullSpace with step map Tq (used by SecondEigenvalue and HalfLineTransfer) and the complex completion GNSHilbert.H with opT (used by GapToOperator). toH sends x to the class of (x, 0): norm_coe_ofPair matches its norm to the GNS norm, inner_coe_ofPair shows the complex pairing is the real form with zero imaginary part, coe_ofPair_eq_zero_iff shows it kills exactly the null space, and opT_coe_ofPair with Transfer.Tq_mk gives intertwines. The gap crosses both ways: gapAt_iff_opT_contracts says GapAt D r holds exactly when opT D contracts the vacuum complement by r, and gapAt_iff_tq_contracts does the same on the real quotient. inner_Tq_nonneg_of_positiveTransfer carries operator positivity to Tq. No gap is produced and none is consumed.
import MassGap.ClayCapstone  -- What the transfer chain delivers, and on which algebra. clay_gap_of_gapAt: from TransferGap.GapAt, for a TransferData with PositiveTransfer, at 0 < r < 1 the GNS transfer operator is self-adjoint with spectrum in {1} union Icc 0 (exp(-Delta)) at Delta = -log r > 0, and 1 its greatest element, with no Rayleigh statement and no subdominant supremum. gaugeInv_clay_gap_of_gapAt and gaugeInv_clay_gap_of_pairing state the same on the gauge-invariant algebra, where reflection positivity and PositiveTransfer restrict for free and a pairing inequality is the only input. transferData_of_state_facts builds on halfSpaceAlg, which contains functions of a single link variable, while StrongCoupling.wilsonCorrConn_abs_le_coreConst_mul_rate_pow bounds a plaquette pair; GaugeInvariantAlgebra supplies the smaller algebra where they meet. absolute_decay_of_form_decay and norm_Tq_pow_mk_mul carry form decay to the GNS quotient.
import MassGap.GaugeInvariantAlgebra  -- The local gauge action on Z^4, and the invariant subalgebra. igaugeTransform g U l = g l.2 * U l * (g (ishift l.1 l.2))^{-1}, and IsIGaugeInvariant quantifies over every gauge function -- the local group, not the centre. transferData_of_state_facts builds on halfSpaceAlg, which contains halfLinkObs (a continuous function of one link variable), so GapAt there demands a connected-correlator bound for observables that are not gauge invariant, while StrongCoupling.wilsonCorrConn_abs_le_coreConst_mul_rate_pow bounds a plaquette pair. gaugeInvHalfSpaceAlg is the smaller algebra: gaugeInvHalfSpaceAlg_le and reflPositiveOn_gaugeInv give reflection positivity by restriction, one_mem_gaugeInvHalfSpaceAlg and gaugeInvHalfSpaceAlg_shift_stable (resting on ishiftConf_igaugeTransform) are the closure facts, and iplaqObs_mem_gaugeInvHalfSpaceAlg places the plaquette observables in it. gaugeInvTransferData is the transfer data on it; gaugeInv_form_pow reads its form against the 2n-th transfer power as the reflected-shifted pairing, and positiveTransfer_gaugeInv / gapAt_gaugeInv_of_gapAt carry positivity and the gap down. gaugeInv_pairing_of_gram and the diagonal-dominance lemmas (gaugeInv_pairing_of_diagDominant, gaugeInv_pairing_of_weighted_diagDominant, gapEntry) reduce the pairing inequality to finite families.
import MassGap.GapToOperator  -- Carrying TransferGap.GapAt to the completed Hilbert space, without inverting anything. GapAt lives on the pre-Hilbert form A while opT lives on the completion, and a bound on a dense subspace is not a bound on the space until continuity is used. norm_opT_le_of_orth carries the contraction to the whole vacuum complement of H by running the inequality on the projected vector y - <Omega,y> Omega, which makes both sides continuous in y; norm_opT_pow_le iterates it to r^n; clustering_opT is Cauchy-Schwarz on top, giving exponential clustering of the completed correlation. opTgap is opT on the vacuum complement and spectrum_opT_subset places the spectrum of opT in {1} union [-r, r]. No functional calculus, no logarithm and no invertibility of T appear. GapAt is a hypothesis throughout.
import MassGap.SlabKernelOperator  -- The slab kernel as a bounded operator on L2, with no integral-operator machinery. A bounded jointly measurable kernel between two probability spaces induces kernelCLM, a ContinuousLinearMap between the L2 spaces, from the two facts the configuration space has -- a finite power of a compact group carrying a probability measure, and a bounded kernel -- at which point Cauchy-Schwarz in MeasureTheory.L2 is the whole argument; no Schatten, Hilbert-Schmidt or trace class is named. abs_kernelFun_le gives the row bound at every x. norm_kernelCLM_le bounds the operator norm by the kernel bound; kernelCLM_nonneg: a nonnegative kernel preserves nonnegativity; kernelCLM_ne_zero: a kernel bounded below by a positive constant gives a nonzero operator. slabHaar is the per-slab Haar measure on SliceTrace's slab configuration spaces (slabVol_eq_pi_slabHaar).
import MassGap.SlabTransferAdjoint  -- The adjoint of a kernel operator, and the quadratic form. adjoint_kernelCLM: (T_K)-dagger = T_{K-transpose}, by one Fubini against a dominating function; isSelfAdjoint_kernelCLM_of_symm for a symmetric kernel on one space. isSelfAdjoint_adjointComp and inner_adjointComp_self_nonneg hold for every bounded operator between two real Hilbert spaces. Operator positivity is reduced to a Gram condition: FiniteGram, inner_kernelCLM_self_nonneg_of_gram, inner_kernelCLM_self_nonneg_of_finiteGram. At a constant kernel the quadratic form is evaluated (inner_kernelCLM_self_of_const) and zero_mem_spectrum_of_apply_eq_zero puts 0 in the spectrum when a nonzero vector is killed. KernelStochastic: a stochastic kernel fixes the constant (kernelCLM_oneLp_eq_oneLp).
